  "query_preview": "..(Tonight Through Tuesday)\nIssued at 948 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n04Z upper level analysis has weak ridging across the northern and\ncentral Plains with sharp trof digging across the Pacific\nNorthwest. Weak shortwave is currently lifting northeast across \nsouth central WY, with some light shower and thunderstorm activity\nnoted. Skies are currently partly cloudy across the forecast \narea. At the surface, high pressure is in place across the eastern\nDakotas, with a weak low pressure center over central Wyoming. In\nbetween these two surface features, light southeast return flow \nis underway - with wind speeds mainly in the 10-15 mph range. \nTemperatures across the forecast area at 10pm are mainly in the \n50s to lower 60s.\n\nWeak shortwave across south central Wyoming will continue to lift\nnortheast into the forecast area after midnight. Some isolated \nlight rain showers and some isolated thunder are expected from the\nBlack Hills area and east through the early morning hours. \nSoutheast boundary layer flow may become a bit breezy after \nmidnight, subsiding a bit by early Wednesday morning. Low \ntemperatures are expected to dip into the upper 40s to mid 50s by \nsunrise.\n\nFor Wednesday...upper shortwave will shift east of the area by mid\nmorning, with skies clearing across much of the area by mid day. A\nweak frontal boundary will slide through western areas by late\nmorning and early afternoon, switching light southerly winds \naround to out of the north. Thermal ridge rebounds across the \narea late in the day, allowing temperatures to climb into the \nupper 70s to mid 80s by late in the afternoon. Increasing clouds\nahead of the next weather system will keep temperatures mild \nWednesday night.\n\nStrong upper low develops in the western trof across the West\nCoast region on Thursday, with Northern Plains ridge axis \nshifting east during the day. A strong upper shortwave is progged\nto lift through the forecast area during peak heating late \nThursday afternoon into Thursday evening. With modest east to \nsoutheast surface return flow bringing moisture back into the area\nduring the day, and decent low level shear, there could be a few \nstronger storms that develop across far southwestern portions of \nthe area. These showers and storms should continue to shift \nnortheast across the area during the late afternoon and evening\nhours, with best chances for significant rainfall across \nsouthwest into south central South Dakota into Thursday night. \nMany locations across this area could see a quarter to half an \ninch of precipitation from these showers and storms, with locally\nhigher amounts possible. With ample cloud cover and precip across\nmuch of the area, temperatures will be cooler, in the 60s to mid \n70s on Thursday.\n\nClosed upper low shifts into the Northern Rockies on Friday, with\nweak ridging moving back into the forecast area. Friday will be \nmostly dry, with temperatures rebounding into the 70s. By Friday \nnight and for the weekend, the upper level low will traverse the \nNorthern Plains, bringing widespread light moisture, and cooler \ntemperatures to the area. If this pattern and track of the upper \nlow holds true, many locations could see an additional quarter to \nhalf an inch of precipitation through early Monday.\n\nMainly dry and mild conditions return for the first half of next\nweek.",
